It works when trying to perform the amova using this distance matrix, but what really concerns me is that I was trying to compute the amova using different missing options ("ignore" and "loci"). My question is that if I give the distance matrix, then those distances are the ones used to compute the amova, and I don't know how it works the function dist() with the missing data present in my dataset.

Could you explain me a little bit about that? I know that by default poppr.amova computes the diss.dist between pairs of individuals (allele differences between the two genotypes), but I don't understand how it works in the case of the Euclidean distance (how we could obtain a numeric euclidean distance from the alleles of the genotypes? Is it something intrinsic of the genind object?)

Thanks

Carles

